          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            the commutative product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            is replaced by a noncommutative associative product. This is often called a
            %strong star product
            and denoted “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
            ”.
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/archetypal+example"} archetypal example
            is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moyal+star+product"} Moyal star product
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MoyalStarProduct"}
            below) that deforms the function algebra on a Poisson vector space, and often “star product” is by default understood to be a Moyal star product. Indeed every star product induced from a constant rank-2 tensor on a vector space is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            to a Moyal star product (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SymmetricContribution"}
            below).
          %p More recently also nonassociative “star products” have been proposed to be of interest.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#on_finitedimensional_vector_spaces On finite-dimensional vector spaces
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional vector space
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \in V \otimes V
            be an element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            (not necessarily skew symmetric at the moment).
          %p
            We may canonically regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            is canonically regarded as a constant rank-2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            by forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            on the tensor product of the space of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi
                  \\;\colon\;
                  C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  C^\infty(V) \otimes C^\infty(V)
                  \\,.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\partial_i\}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(T V)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
